<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/how-it-works/market-lifecycle.mdx">
---
title: Market lifecycle
---

Every RetroPick market follows the **same basic story**. Only the **question** and **how oracle data is combined** change between market types.

1. A market **opens** with a defined question and settlement conditions.
2. You **deposit** into the outcome you believe will happen.
3. At a specific time, the system **locks**: rules stop changing.
4. At settlement, **authorized market data** is read automatically.
5. The **result is calculated** and **winners** are paid from the pool according to the rules.
6. **No one** can change the outcome after the market has **locked**.

## Voids (refunds)

Sometimes the rules say there is **no fair winner**—for example an exact tie on a boundary in a direction market, or a tolerance band in a relative-value market. Then the market may **void**: stakes are returned instead of paying one side the whole pot.

See **Market types** for per-template void behavior.

## Technical note

On-chain, a **template** defines the market type, feeds, and parameters. An **epoch** (or “round”) moves through open → lock → resolve → claim phases. The implementation uses Chainlink-family feeds and audited contracts; see **Technical overview**.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/how-it-works/trading-flow.mdx">
---
title: Trading flow
---

## Plain-language walkthrough

1. **Choose a market** — Read the headline and the resolution text. Make sure you know what “winning” means.
2. **Pick a side** — Often two outcomes; some templates have more buckets or tiers.
3. **Add funds** — Your stake joins the pool for that outcome. Prices can reflect what the crowd believes; they are not a guarantee.
4. **Wait for lock** — After lock, the rules are fixed.
5. **Settlement** — The protocol reads feeds and applies the rule you saw up front.
6. **Payout or void** — Winners receive from the combined pools; voids return stakes per the rules. You may need a **claim** step depending on the product version.

## Who pays whom

Generally, **losers’ stakes fund winners** (after fees). Your share depends on how much you put in and how the contract splits the pool for that template—especially in weighted tiers (e.g. ladder markets).

### Technical note

Positions are represented on-chain; settlement may involve resolver modules, conditional tokens, and explicit `claim` calls. See `currentSmartContract.md` in the repo for exact mechanics.
</file>

