<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/cascade.mdx">
---
title: Cascade
---

**Question:** How many **stacked** support or resistance **levels** were **broken** during the round?

You pick a **tier** for “how many breaks.” **More breaks** can map to **higher** tiers with different payoffs—similar spirit to ladder weighting.

## How it settles

Using **extreme** price through the epoch (e.g. **high** for upward stacks), the contract counts how many defined levels were **broken** and selects the matching tier.

## When to use it

Trend or **breakout** strength—when you expect a run through multiple **obvious** levels, not just a quiet close.

### Technical note

Cascade shares the **OHLC / path** DNA with corridor-style resolution in the engineering doc; read `currentSmartContract.md` for feed and reporting details.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/composite.mdx">
---
title: Composite
---

**Question:** Do **multiple** independent conditions end up **true** together under a fixed logic rule?

Up to **four** conditions can each use its own feed/threshold. Rules are typically **And**, **Or**, or **Majority**.

## How it settles

Each condition is evaluated from its oracle reading. The configured logic decides whether **Yes** or **No** wins.

## When to use it

Macro scenarios where **several** things must line up—risk-on packages, joint targets, correlated moves.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/convergence.mdx">
---
title: Convergence
---

**Question:** Are two assets **closer together** or **further apart** at the end vs at lock?

You are betting on the **relationship** between two feeds, not on either asset in isolation.

## How it settles

**Lock** records the spread (or ratio). **Settlement** reads both feeds again. If the gap **narrowed**, **Converge** wins; if it **widened**, **Diverge** wins. If the change is **within a defined tolerance**, the market may **void** and refund.

## When to use it

Relative-value views—e.g. whether ETH **outperforms** BTC **this period** expressed as spread movement.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/corridor.mdx">
---
title: Corridor
---

**Question:** Did price **stay inside** an upper/lower band for the **entire** round?

Unlike “close in a bucket,” corridor cares about **intraday** extremes—if price **tags** either boundary, that can decide the outcome.

## How it settles

**OHLC-style** extremes (high/low across the epoch) are compared to the band. **Inside** wins if **both** high and low respected the corridor; **Outside** wins if either boundary was touched or crossed (per template).

## When to use it

Range-bound **behavior** over time—not just where the candle **closes**.

### Technical note

Corridor often relies on **TrustedReporter** / event-oracle **OHLC** paths feeding resolved highs and lows—see the contract reference.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/direction.mdx">
---
title: Direction
---

**Question:** Will this asset finish **higher or lower** than a **reference price** taken at lock?

## How it settles

At **lock**, a price snapshot is stored. At the **end** of the round, the feed is read again. **Up** wins if the final price is **above** the lock price; **Down** wins if **below**. If the two readings are **exactly equal**, the market **voids** and deposits are refunded.

## Example

- Lock: ETH **$2,500** · Close: **$2,680** → **Up** wins.

## When to use it

You have a simple short-term directional view and want a two-outcome market without extra thresholds or zones.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/index.mdx">
---
title: Market types
---

RetroPick ships **nine** market **templates**. Each asks a different question; settlement is always **data-driven** (see **Oracles & fairness**).

| Type | Question in one line |
|------|----------------------|
| **Direction** | Up or down vs a saved snapshot? |
| **Threshold** | At or above a level, or below? |
| **Range close** | Which price **zone** at close? |
| **Velocity** | **How much** did it move (either direction)? |
| **Ladder** | Which **tier** at close, with **weighted** payouts? |
| **Convergence** | Are two assets **closer** or **further apart**? |
| **Composite** | Do **multiple** conditions combine to true? |
| **Corridor** | Did price **stay inside** a band the whole time? |
| **Cascade** | How many **stacked levels** were **broken**? |

Use the pages in this section for examples, void rules, and when each type fits your view.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/ladder.mdx">
---
title: Ladder
---

**Question:** Which **tier** will price close in when **tiers have different payout weights**?

Like range-close, but **contrarian** or **rarer** tiers can carry **higher multipliers**.

## How it settles

The winning tier is whichever band contains the **close**. Payouts scale by each tier’s **weight** so harder calls can pay more.

## When to use it

You want **risk/reward** to reflect how difficult your zone is—not every bucket pays the same.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/range-close.mdx">
---
title: Range close
---

**Question:** Which **price bucket** will the asset **close in**?

The range is split into **zones**. Only the zone that contains the **final** price wins; **intraday** path does not matter.

## How it settles

At settlement, the closing price falls into exactly one bucket. Everyone on that bucket shares the pool from all other buckets.

## When to use it

Range-bound or **volatility-soft** views—you expect price to **finish in a band**, not necessarily blow out in one direction.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/threshold.mdx">
---
title: Threshold
---

**Question:** Will a published number at settlement be **at or above** a fixed level—or **below** it?

The number can be a **price**, **staking APR**, **macro** statistic, **NAV-style** value, and more—depending on the configured feed.

## How it settles

The market is created with a **threshold**. At settlement, the oracle value is compared to that line. **At or above** wins if the reading meets or beats the threshold; otherwise **Below** wins (exact tie-breaking and voids follow the template rules).

## When to use it

You care about a **specific level** rather than direction—yields, macro prints, or “will we cross $X?” style views.

### Technical note

Threshold absorbs several **legacy** threshold-style types; effective thresholds can use **anchor** prices when configured. See `currentSmartContract.md` for **oracle classes** (price, rate, SmartData, macro, …).
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/market-types/velocity.mdx">
---
title: Velocity
---

**Question:** **How much** will price move from the start—**ignoring direction**?

A **+3%** and **-3%** move can land in the **same bucket** because only **magnitude** matters.

## How it settles

Lock stores a **starting price**. At settlement, **absolute** percent change from that start is computed and matched to **movement buckets** (e.g. 0–1%, 1–3%, …).

## When to use it

You expect a **big move** from an event but are **not** sure which way—data prints, liquidations, headline risk.
</file>

