<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/faq.mdx">
---
title: FAQ & safety
---

## Is this financial advice?

No. These docs explain **how the mechanism works**, not what you should buy or sell. Prediction markets are **risky**; you can lose your entire stake.

## What if the UI or a link looks wrong?

- Use only **official** RetroPick interfaces.
- **Verify** contract addresses for your network using authoritative deployment info.
- Never paste your **seed phrase** anywhere.

## Testnet vs mainnet

Deployments on **testnets** use tokens with **no real-world value**. Treat **mainnet** as real money at risk.

## Who do I contact for bugs?

Follow your deployment’s official support channels (Discord, email, or issue tracker). This documentation site may lag the latest release—**on-chain rules and the market page text** are the source of truth for a given market.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/index.mdx">
---
title: Welcome
---

RetroPick is a **prediction market** platform: you take a side on how a clearly defined question will resolve when the round ends. Outcomes are settled using **automated market data** (Chainlink-style feeds)—not by someone picking a winner by hand.

These docs are for **everyone**: you do not need a deep crypto background to understand how trading and payouts work. Where it helps, we add short **technical notes** for wallet users and developers.

## What you will find here

- **Getting started** — what you need, wallets, and network basics
- **How it works** — lifecycle from open market through lock, settlement, payout, and voids
- **Market types** — all nine RetroPick templates in plain language
- **Oracles & fairness** — why the result is hard to tamper with
- **Technical overview** — glossary and protocol outline (points to the full contract reference in the repo)

## Source material

This site is aligned with the same concepts as the repository’s `.marketTypes.md` (market types) and `package/contract/currentSmartContract.md` (Solidity-level detail). If anything disagrees with shipped code, **the code wins**.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/oracles-and-fairness.mdx">
---
title: Oracles & fairness
---

## In simple terms

RetroPick markets do not rely on a person typing in the winning price. At settlement, the protocol reads from **authorized data feeds**—the same family of **Chainlink** infrastructure used widely in DeFi—so the number is consistent, timestamped, and expensive to fake at scale.

## What this means for you

- The **resolution criteria** are fixed after **lock**.
- The **data source** is known up front (shown in product and in contract parameters for a template).
- The outcome is **computed** from those readings using the rule printed on the market—**not** by moderator vote.

## Edge cases

Feeds can misbehave; templates define **void** conditions for ties or invalid combinations of readings. When in doubt, read the market’s own resolution copy.

### Technical note

RetroPick supports multiple Chainlink **oracle classes** (e.g. price, rate, SmartData, macro) and **TrustedReporter** paths where configured. The Solidity reference (`currentSmartContract.md`) documents checkpoints **A/B**, multi-feed **Composite** reads, and **OHLC** paths for corridor/cascade-style markets.
</file>

<file path="apps/docs/contents/docs/technical-overview.mdx">
---
title: Technical overview
---

This page is a **compact map** of the on-chain system. For line-by-line behavior, gas tables, and migration notes, use the canonical file in the repository:

**`package/contract/currentSmartContract.md`**

## Glossary

- **Template** — Market definition keyed by `templateId` (type, feeds, fees, execution mode).
- **Epoch** — One cycle: open → lock → resolve → claim (often called a **round** in the product).
- **Lock** — Rules and timing are fixed; trading on the outcome typically stops or is restricted per product rules.
- **Checkpoint A / B** — Oracle samples at lock and/or resolve used by resolvers (meanings depend on market type).
- **Manual vs rolling** — **Manual**: keepers drive `openEpoch` / `lockEpoch` / `resolveEpoch`. **Rolling**: pipeline advances rounds on a schedule with fewer discrete actions.
- **Dispatcher + modules** — The live engine uses a **UUPS proxy** with a **dispatcher** boundary; some selectors are **root-owned** (user ops, claims, admin) and others delegate into modules—see the doc above for the current matrix.

## Fees and reserves

Protocol fees and reserves (treasury withdrawal, fee buckets, yield router interactions) are implementation-specific. The contract doc explains **`feeReserveTotal`**, **`claimsReserveTotal`**, and **`withdrawFees`** semantics.

## What to read next

1. **Market types** (this site) — user-facing semantics and void rules.
2. **`currentSmartContract.md`** — operator narrative, resolver notes, and **MarketType** consolidation/migration history.
</file>

